#
# The RPM %preun scriptlet, named by CPACK_RPM_PRE_UNINSTALL_SCRIPT_FILE. CPack
# copies its contents into the generated spec, so this file is the body of %preun.
#
# THE ARGUMENT AGAIN MEANS A COUNT, and in %preun it counts what will remain
# AFTER this transaction: 0 is the last copy going away - a real uninstall - and
# 1 is an upgrade, where a newer copy is about to take this one's place. So the
# service is stopped and disabled only at 0. Stopping it at 1 would turn every
# upgrade into an outage, and %post's try-restart already puts the new binary
# into service.
#
# This is what the %systemd_preun macro does on a distribution that has it. It is
# written out longhand because CPack generates the spec and does not expand
# distribution macros for us.

set -e

if [ "$1" -eq 0 ]; then
   if [ -d /run/systemd/system ] && command -v systemctl >/dev/null 2>&1; then
      # Stopped, not killed: SIGTERM brings main.cpp out of its wait loop and the
      # server drains for ShutdownDrainSeconds before exiting.
      systemctl --no-reload disable hmailserver.service >/dev/null 2>&1 || :
      systemctl stop hmailserver.service >/dev/null 2>&1 || :
   fi

   cat <<'MESSAGE'

hMailServer has been removed. Deliberately left in place:

    /etc/hmailserver        the configuration, with your database credentials
    /var/lib/hmailserver    the mail store - every message on this server
    /var/log/hmailserver    the logs, which record who was sent what

None of it is reconstructible and none of it is the package manager's to decide
about. The database is untouched for the same reason. The hmailserver user and
group are kept as well, so that what is left keeps an owner with a name.

Remove them by hand when you are certain:

    rm -rf /etc/hmailserver /var/lib/hmailserver /var/log/hmailserver
    userdel hmailserver

MESSAGE
fi

exit 0
